In the \(\mathrm{FeCoMOF-P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , three M1 centers and three \(\mathrm{ox}^{2-}\) ligands are fused together to form a three-membered triangular ring. Additionally, there are M1-M2 dimers (connected through an oxygen atom) that are further connected through oxalate anions to form a hexagonal twelve-membered Fe/Co ring with alternating M1 and M2 centers. The triangular and hexagonal rings are fused to form a Kagomé-type Fe/Co-ox layer by sharing O atoms of the \(\mathrm{ox}^{2-}\) ligands. These Kagomé layers are further connected by Pbpy ligands that are almost perpendicular to the layers, giving rise to hexagonal and triangular channels perpendicular to the layers. The hexagonal channels are formed around the \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) polyoxoanions that act as templates (Figure 1). The size of the hexagonal channels formed by the twelve-membered rings (internal diameter of \(12.293\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , Figure S4, Supporting Information) matches the diameter of the \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) polyoxoanion \((11.0\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , Figure S5, Supporting Information), leading to an effective encapsulation. This perfect fit between the size of the pores and of the \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , results in a compact structure with an extremely short \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Co}\) distance of only \(4.99\,\mathring\mathrm{A}\) (Figure S6, Supporting Information), which is in favor of the directional transport of electrons in \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) .[42] Meanwhile, the size of the hole in the three-membered ring (with
an internal diameter of \({\approx}3.42\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , Figure S7, Supporting Information) is exactly suitable for \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) molecules (diameter of \(3.1\,\mathring\mathrm{A}\) ). Therefore, this kind of structure is advantageous to accelerate the transport of electrons and reactants, and the e-NRR process, as we will see below.
For comparison, we synthesize three \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F}\) with similar composition but without \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , formulated as FeMOF, CoMOF and FeCoMOF (see Supporting Information for synthetic details). These compounds present a 2D structure with small tetragonal channels, showing that the templating \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) are pivotal for the synthesis of the previously described 3D networks with the large hexagonal channels. These \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F}\) are isostructural, as shown by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns (Figure S17, Supporting Information). However, we could only obtain single crystals of the FeMOF. The asymmetric unit of FeMOF contains two \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{II})\) ions (Fe1 and Fe2), two oxalate dianion and two and a half Pbpy ligands, all located on general positions (Figure S8, Supporting Information). Both Fe atoms show an octahedral trans- \(\mathrm{FeO}_{2}\mathrm{N}_{2}\) coordination environment. The Fe1 center is surrounded by two O2 and two O3 atoms (from two oxalate ligands) and two N1 atoms (from two Pbpy ligands), whereas the Fe2 center is surrounded by two O1 and two O4 atoms (from two oxalate ligands) and two N2 atoms (from two Pbpy ligands, Figure S9, Supporting Information). These coordination environments are similar to those observed in Fe and Co atoms of \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) as described above. As shown in Figure S10 (Supporting Information), the \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{II})\) ions and the \(\mathrm{ox}^{2-}\) anions form a neutral \(\{\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{ox})\}_{\mathrm{n}}\) regular chain along the \(b\) direction. Adjacent \(\{\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{ox})\}_{\mathrm{n}}\) chains are connected by slightly bent and almost orthogonal Pbpy ligands to form a \([\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{Pbpy})(\mathrm{ox})]\) layer in the ab plane containing two different rectangular holes with dimensions of \(15.07\,\times\,3.49\) and \(15.07\times7.04\mathrm{~\AA}^{2}\) . The valence-bond calculations[54] and XPS spectra (Figure S11, Supporting Information) show that both Fe
atoms are in the \(+2\) oxidation states, in agreement with the 1:1 \(\mathrm{Fe:ox^{2-}}\) stoichiometry.
The PXRD patterns of the five \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) and the three \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F}\) samples are consistent with the simulated ones from the single crystal X-ray structures (Figures S12–S17, Supporting Information), further confirming the structure and purity of all the prepared samples. Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) spectra of the five \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) samples and \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) are shown in Figure S18 (Supporting Information). The FTIR spectrum of pure \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) shows four characteristic bands at 965, 912, 794, and \(1095~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) that are attributed to \(\nu(\mathrm{W}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{d}})\) , \(\nu(\mathrm{W}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{b}}{\cdot}\mathrm{W})\) , \(\nu(\mathrm{W}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{c}}{\cdot}\mathrm{W})\) and \(\nu(\mathrm{P}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})\) vibrations, respectively.[43] The five \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) samples also show similar peak positions. In addition, these \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) samples show bands in the \(1674{-}1165~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) region, attributed to the Pppy and \(\mathrm{ox^{2-}}\) ligands. The FTIR spectra further supports the composition of all the synthesized materials.
