## 3. Electrochemical Performances for e-NRR
The e-NRR activity of electrodes prepared with the \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F}\) samples were tested in \(0.1\,\mathrm{~M~}\) HCl \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) -saturated). The electrodes were prepared as catalyst-modified carbon cloth (CC) electrodes and were directly used as the working electrodes. The performances of the catalysts are tested using a typical three-electrode system with a H-type cell (Figure S30, Supporting Information). Before testing, the argon and nitrogen gas were bubbled into oxidizing trapper \((0.1\,\mathrm{~M~}\,\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}\) solution) and alkaline trapped \((0.1\,\mathrm{~M~}\,\mathrm{KOH})\) to purge the \(\mathrm{NO_{x}}\) in the gas. The UV–vis adsorption spectra of electrolyte indicate that the absence of \(\mathrm{NO_{x}}\) in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) gas (Figures S31 and S32, Supporting Information). We introduce the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) into the cathode chamber of the electrolytic cell for \(30\,\mathrm{\min}\) to saturate the electrolyte. All the potential mentioned in this study are given versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (vs. RHE). The amount of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) produced by the e-NRR is quantified by the ion chromatography, indophenol blue and Nessler's reagent method (Figures S33–S35 and Table S11, Supporting Information).[49] We use Watt and Chrisp method to detect the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (Figure S36, Supporting Information).[50]
Figure 4a shows the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves of FeCoMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) - and Ar-saturated electrolytes. The reduction current density is enhanced under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere when compared with Ar within a potential window of \(-0.2\) to \(-0.7\,\mathrm{~V~}\) (vs. RHE), suggesting that in this potential range, electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) reduction occur. As depicted in the chronoamperometry test (Figure 4b; Figure S37, Supporting Information), the current density of FeCoMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) increases with the increasing working potential. We have tested the e-NRR performances for all the \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) samples (Figure 4c–f, Figures S35,S38–S42 and Table S11, Supporting Information) after electrolysis for \(2\,\mathrm{~h~}\) obtained by the ion chromatography, indophenol blue and Nessler's reagent methods in \(0.1\,\mathrm{~M~}\) HCl electrolyte. As shown in Table S11 (Supporting Information), three methods give very similar results. As can be seen in Figures S38–S42 (Supporting Information), the highest \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yields rates are obtained at \(-0.4\,\mathrm{~V~}\) (vs. RHE) and as can be seen in Figure 4e,f and Table S11 (Supporting Information), the FeCoMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) sample provides higher \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield rate and FE \((47.04~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) and \(31.76\%\) , respectively) than the other bimetallic samples: \(\mathrm{Fe_{2}C o M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) \((32.21~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) and \(20.25\%\) ) and \(\mathrm{FeCo_{2}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) \((22.75~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) and \(14.55\%\) ) and much higher than the homometallic samples \(\mathrm{FeMOF-P_{2}W_{18}}\) \((17.45~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) and \(13.69\%\) ) and CoMOFP \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) \((16.07~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}\) and \(11.09\%\) ). Furthermore, the excellent performances of sample \(\mathrm{FeCoMOF-P_{2}W_{18}}\) are better than those of many electrocatalysts in aqueous electrolytes (Table S12, Supporting Information). Notably, when the applied potential is over \(-0.4\,\mathrm{~V~}\) (vs. RHE), both the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield rates and
FEs show a decrease that may be caused by competitive adsorption of nitrogen and \(\mathrm{H^{+}/H_{2}}\) on the FeCoMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) material.[51]
The much lower \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield rates and FEs shown by the two homometallic samples (FeMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) and CoMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\)) strongly support the idea that there is a synergistic effect between Fe and Co in the bimetallic \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) materials. To further confirm this synergistic effect of Fe and Co and check the need of the \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) in these catalyst systems for e-NRR, we have also tested the catalytic activity of \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) and of the three \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F}\) samples (that do not contain the \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\)). As shown in Figure 4e,f and Figures S43–S45 (Supporting information), the performance of the bimetallic FeCoMOF sample is much lower than that of the FeCoMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) sample, indicating that the \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) POM is needed to obtain a good catalyst for e-NRR. Furthermore, as expected, the FeCoMOF sample shows a higher \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield rate \((12.14~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat}}^{-1})\) and FE \((11.85\%)\) than those of the two homometallic samples FeMOF \((8.69~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat}}^{-1}\) and \(7.66\%\) ) and CoMOF \((8.85~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{cat}}^{-1}\) and \(77\%\) ). This result further confirms the importance of the \(\mathrm{Fe/Co}\) synergy. Also as expected, the pure \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) sample shows an almost negligible amount of ammonia production (Figure S46, Supporting Information), indicating that pure \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) has almost no e-NRR activity. The comparison of the e-NRR performances of \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\), \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F}\) and pure \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\), leads to the following conclusions: 1) The Co and Fe are the catalytic active centers in the \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) rather than the \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\). 2) There is a strong electron interaction and synergy between Co and Fe in the e-NRR, as described by the analysis of XPS spectra; 3) The \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) in the \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) materials plays an important role in accelerating and facilitating the electrons transfer in the e-NRR process.
We use electrochemical double layer capacitance \(\left(\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)\) to assess the electrochemical active surface area (ECSA), which can further explain the enhanced e-NRR activity.[52] The cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves are measured in the \(450{-}550\;\mathrm{mV}\) range at sweep rates varying from 5 to \(100\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) (Figure 5a; Figure S47, Supporting Information). The result shows that: i) FeCoMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) exhibits a larger \(\mathrm{C_{dl}}\) value \((1.22\;\mathrm{mF\;cm}^{-2})\) than the other \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) compounds \((\mathrm{FeMOF-P_{2}W_{18}}=0.84\;\mathrm{mF\;cm}^{-2}\), \(\mathrm{Fe_{2}C o M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\ =\ 1.14\ \ \mathrm{mF}\ \ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), \(\mathrm{FeCo_{2}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\ =\ 1.10\ \ \mathrm{mF}\ \ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) and \(\mathrm{CoMOF-P_{2}W_{18}}\ =\ 0.88\ \ \mathrm{mF}\ \ \mathrm{cm}^{-2})\), and
Information), and an associative distal e-NRR pathway on the FeCoMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) catalyst surface.[55] Figures S53–S59 (Supporting Information) show that \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) are neither detected during the electrolytic processes for FeMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , CoMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{CoMOF-P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , \(\mathrm{FeCo_{2}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) , CoMOF, FeCoMOF and FeMOF.
The electrocatalysts stability is a very important parameter to evaluate. After immersing the five \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) compounds in HCl or KOH aqueous solution \((0.1\,\mathrm{~M})\) for \(48\,\mathrm{~h~}\) or keeping them in an oven at \(200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2~\mathrm{{h}}\) , the PXRD patterns of the five \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) compounds remain unchanged, indicating that compounds \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) have high chemical and thermal stabilities (Figures S60–S69, Supporting Information). These FTIR spectra further proves the above conclusion (Figure S70, Supporting Information). In addition, we re-measured the crystal data of a single crystal of compound FeCoMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) that was soaked in an aqueous solution for more than a year and found that it also exhibited a good diffraction pattern (Figure S71, Supporting Information). In addition, consecutive recycling tests were performed at \(-0.4\:\mathrm{V}\) (vs. RHE) for the five \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) compounds in a \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) -saturated \(0.1\,\textsf{M}\) HCl electrolyte. Figure 7a and Figure S72 (Supporting Information) show the stationary chronoamperometry curves of the five \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) compounds at \(-0.4\mathrm{~V~}\) (vs. RHE) for consecutive 5 cycling tests. Figure 7b,c and Figures S73–S77 (Supporting Information) show that the five \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) samples have stable \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield rates and FEs (the corresponding UV–vis absorption spectra are tested by using the indophenol blue and Nessler's reagent methods). When varying the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) flow
rates, FeCoMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) shows stable \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield rates and FEs at \(-0.4\:\mathrm{V}\) (vs. RHE), showing that \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) diffusion is a non-rate-determining step (Figure S78, Supporting Information). Moreover, the current densities of the five \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) compounds show no variation in \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) of e-NRR process and there are no significant changes for the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield and FEs after the \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) test (Figure 7d; Figure S79, Supporting Information). The XRD patterns and FTIR spectra of \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) after \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) electrocatalytic testing do not change before and after the electrocatalytic reaction (Figure S80, Supporting Information), further proving the stability of the five \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) catalysts.
## 4. Understanding the Possible Mechanism in Catalytic Activity
To gain in-depth insight into the effect of \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) and metals sites (Fe or Co) on e-NRR efficiency, CV experiments and DFT calculations have been performed. The CV experiments of the pure \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) (tetrabutylammonium salt), three \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F}\) and three \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) samples (FeMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , FeCoMOF\(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) and CoMOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) ) have been studied in \(0.1\,\textsf{M}\,\mathrm{N}_{2}\) -saturated HCl electrolyte. The CV curves of the \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F}\) samples all show two reduction peaks (0.20 and \(-0.41\mathrm{~V~}\) vs. RHE in FeMOF), (0.21 and \(-0.40\;\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE in FeCoMOF) and (0.21 and \(-0.41\mathrm{~V~}\) vs. RHE in Co-MOF), corresponding to the reduction process of Co or Fe (Figure S81, Supporting Information). The reduction peaks of pure \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) are observed at 0.32, \(-0.23\) , \(-0.39\) and \(-0.64\mathrm{~V~}\) versus RHE. It is worth noting that
the reduction peaks of pure \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) are clearly more positive than those of \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F}\) (the second reduction peaks, \(-0.41\) , 0.40 and \(-0.41\;\mathrm{V}\) ), indicating that the pure \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) get electrons preferentially.
In the \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F-P_{2}W_{18}}\) compounds, the reduction peaks at 0.35 and \(-0.18\;\mathrm{V}\) (CoMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) ), 0.33 and \(-0.20\;\mathrm{V}\) (FeCoMOF\(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\)) and 0.40 and \(-0.20\;\mathrm{V}\) (FeMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\)) correspond to the electron storage of the \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) (Figure S81, Supporting Information). In contrast, the broad reduction peaks at \({\approx}0.43\,\mathrm{~V~}\) \((\mathrm{CoMOF-P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18})\) , -0.42 V \((\mathrm{FeCoMOF-P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18})\) and \(-0.42\mathrm{~\,~V~}\) \((\mathrm{FeMOF-P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18})\) , correspond to the overlap of the last reduction peak of the \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F}\) and the middle reduction peak of the \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , suggesting that there is a strong electron transfer between the \(\mathrm{Fe_{x}C o_{y}M O F}\) and the \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) . Additionally, we have studied the CV curves of FeMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , FeCoMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) and CoMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) at different scan rates (25 to \(125\,\mathrm{\mV\s^{-1}}\) (Figures S82a,S83a and S84a, Supporting Information). These studies (Figures S82b,S83b and S84b, Supporting Information) show that the redox peak currents increase linearly with the square root of the scan rates, indicating that the electrochemical process is a diffusion-controlled process.[56,57]
To explore the electron storage and transfer behavior in the electrochemical process of FeMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , FeCoMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) and CoMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , we have conducted a series of Kohn-Sham DFT calculations using the standard B3LYP functional (Figure S85 and Table S14, Supporting Information). Figure 8a compares the orbital energies and composition of FeMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , FeCoMOF
\(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) and CoMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) with those of the pure \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) . It is noteworthy that the involvement of \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) induces obviously different lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) and highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO). The \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) has a lower LUMO energy than FeMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , FeCoMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) and CoMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) . This fact implies that the electrons will preferentially be localized on the \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) units during the electrochemical reduction process. Only when the LUMO orbital of \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) is filled with electrons \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) is fully reduced), then the electrons will be transferred into the MOFs LUMO. Considering the HOMO orbital, we can see that FeCoMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) has a higher HOMO energy than FeMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) and CoMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , indicating that FeCoMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) has a stronger reduction ability. Such calculation indicates that in the electrocatalytic process, \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) receives the electrons and acts as an electron reservoir that, once filled with electrons, will transfers them to the dual-metal-site of FeCoMOF- \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) . The higher HOMO energy of the catalysts with two different metallic sites \((\mathrm{FeCoMOF-P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18})\) is responsible for its higher e-NRR catalytic activity due to its stronger reducing power.
