In situ Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy was performed to further investigate the intermediate species on the FeCoMOF- \(\mathsf{P}_{2}\mathsf{W}_{18}\) during the e-NRR. Figure 9d shows the in situ FTIR spectra of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) passed through the electrolyte at different potentials. With increasing potentials, the peak intensity at \(1120~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (corresponding to the \(\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{N}\) stretching bond) gradually increases, indicating that the \(\mathrm{N}=\mathrm{N}\) triple bond is broken and \(-\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{y}}\) \((1\leq\mathrm{y}\leq4)\) generated on the surface.[58] As shown in Figure 9d,e, the peaks at 1420 and \(1690~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are caused by the asymmetric and symmetric deformation vibrations of \(\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}\), respectively. The bands at 1510/1550, 1330, and \(3230~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are attributed to the H-N-H and \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) wagging and N-H stretching of adsorbed \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{y}}\) species, respectively.[59] Because the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{y}}\) was not detected in the products of e-NRR (Figure 6d; Figure S51, Supporting Information), it can be deduced that the e-NRR proceeds following an associative distal mode.
